WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.500
The end of an era has arrived.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.161
No Time to Die will be Daniel Craig's final
foray as British super-spy James Bond, his

00:00:07.161 --> 00:00:12.070
fifth Bond film in 14 years, making him the
longest-serving man to play the role.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:15.690
Let's break down the trailer for this Bond's
final bow.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.160
Bond and Dr. Madeleine Swann's drive off into
the sunset at the end of Spectre was apparently

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.269
the only retirement announcement MI6 was ever
going to get.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:27.750
Bond took off to Jamaica to live an idyllic
existence on the water.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.699
He's been there with Swann for five years,
with his colleagues believing he's dead.

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:37.200
From the end of 2015's Spectre to the beginning
of 2020's No Time to Die - the real-life and

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.940
fictional timelines basically align here - life
has gone on without Bond, exactly as he wanted.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:48.270
It's given Lashana Lynch's Nomi the opportunity
to replace him as a 00 agent and acquire a

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.330
significant amount of experience.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:55.520
Nomi also appears to take on a more militaristic,
tactical style for clandestine operations

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.219
compared to Bond's suave tuxedo look.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:00.140
Dr. Swann is hiding something.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.870
"Why would I betray you?"

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.469
"We all have our secrets."

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:08.000
The No Time to Die trailer suggests that Bond
and Madeleine will break up following that

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.000
betrayal.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.860
Considering her father was Mr. White, the
assassinated Spectre ally, and that she was

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.500
very aware of Spectre's existence, she no
doubt has an insidious history she's worked

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.500
to bury.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.640
If she knew about her father's criminal ties,
was she possibly more of a party to it than

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.250
she originally led on?

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:29.170
It's also possible Dr. Swann has some connection
to Safin, the villain played by Rami Malek,

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.479
because she's been sent the same mask he wears.

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:35.670
Could Bond be trying to avoid re-experiencing
the heartbreak Vesper Lynd put him through

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.369
at the end of Casino Royale?

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:43.290
At the end of Spectre, Bond relinquishes Spectre
leader Ernst Stavro Blofeld, played by Christoph

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.110
Waltz, to MI6 head M for arrest.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.649
Now in custody and under what appears to be
solitary confinement, Blofeld is a highly

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:51.659
important prisoner.

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:54.730
"He's the most valuable asset this country
has."

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:58.990
But every good supervillain knows how to turn
the screws while in captivity until the moment

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:03.090
they can once more act independently, and
that cunning is what draws us back to character

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.460
archetypes like his again and again.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.879
Hannibal Lecter became a movie icon from a
prison cell, after all.

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:14.090
M asks Bond to not lose control in Blofeld's
presence, given all the horrors Blofeld has

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.690
orchestrated and inflicted upon Bond's life.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.959
Bond says he would never do that, but then
turns the corner to find Dr. Swann with a

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:24.239
folder clearly suggesting a psychological
profile.

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:28.000
They haven't seen each other in what seems
like a while and now meet here, preparing

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.290
to speak with Blofeld.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:34.430
Blofeld knows Dr. Swann's secret, too, and
weaponizing it against Bond is surely a master

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.310
stroke in a grand plan.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:41.230
It's been a while since we've seen CIA agent
Felix Leiter in the James Bond franchise.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.660
The character has been a staple of Bond franchise
films since the first film, Dr. No, but the

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.940
version played by Jeffrey Wright hasn't appeared
since 2008's Quantum of Solace, only rating

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.380
a mention in Spectre.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.940
In No Time to Die, Leiter seeks out his now-retired
friend to take an off-the-books assignment.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.440
"I need a favor, brother.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.769
You're the only one I trust for this."

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:02.879
But how does he know Bond isn't dead?

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:04.890
We need answers, Felix.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:10.290
Longtime James Bond franchise producer Barbara
Broccoli describes Rami Malek's Safin as:

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.190
"[...] the one that really gets under Bond's
skin [...] a nasty piece of work."

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.290
He appears in the No Time to Die trailer as
limping and scarred.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.349
He even holds his gun a bit oddly.

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:23.030
His villainous connections aren't clarified
in the trailer, but it's difficult to believe

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:27.390
he doesn't somehow tie back to Spectre and
Blofeld in some as-yet-unknown way.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.310
And some fans have theorized he's really an
updated Dr. No, who was a member of Spectre.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.209
The word "no" is in the title of the movie,
after all…

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:40.140
Either way, Safin clearly sees parallels between
himself and Bond, even saying he could be

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.720
"speaking to [his] own reflection" when monologuing
to the famous agent.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.870
But he sees one big, obvious difference:

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.319
"Only your skills die with your body.

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:52.140
Mine will survive long after I am gone."

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.790
It seems like the stakes in No Time to Die
will involve Bond's own mortality against

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.960
the world-threatening plots that have become
standard for the franchise.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.549
With No Time to Die, Craig is officially finished
playing Bond, and nobody has been officially

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:06.840
announced to replace him.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:11.660
Audiences will be saying goodbye to a specific
iteration of Bond that has transformed a franchise

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.420
we've known for 50 years.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.821
Craig's Bond has gone from being a fresh-faced
rookie in Casino Royale and to basically a

00:04:17.821 --> 00:04:21.709
hermit who actively hates his job because
it has killed his humanity.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:26.250
He's an orphan who has never known familial
connections, except for with one person: Judy

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.700
Dench's M - who died a terrible death right
in front of him.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.130
Vengeance and loss have been key themes through
all the Craig Bond films.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.270
Unlike most of his predecessors' versions
of the character, Craig's James Bond has often

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:41.300
been completely wrong, and has time and again
been portrayed as desperate.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.880
He's no immortal superhero.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.870
He's human, and he asks for help because he
knows exactly where his strengths begin and

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.310
end, as much as it annoys him to admit it.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.150
And he is always, always tired.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.000
That doesn't cheapen his Bond; it makes him
more real.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.780
The No Time to Die trailer sets the stakes
around Bond himself - his life, his friends,

00:04:58.780 --> 00:04:59.780
his love.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.030
The threatened destruction isn't strictly
political or social, it's personal, which

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.800
is really the perfect bookend for Craig's
Bond.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.360
Maybe James Bond does need to die, and in
doing so, represent the humanity that has

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.000
been so central to the character since Casino
Royale.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.960
Fans can find out everything when No Time
to Die opens in theaters April 2, 2020 in

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.710
the U.K. and April 8, 2020 in the U.S.

